Revision history for Archive-Multics

0.07  2026-10-06
      - Transfer files may carry a "-sha256 <digest>" line, as
        encode_base64 now writes by default: with "-dense9 N" (in either
        order) or alone, before base64 of byte8 data (an archive, or a
        gzip file), with or without a "-byte8" line. The digest of the
        octets carried is checked; an unknown or repeated header line,
        or both -byte8 and -dense9, is an error. --import, t and x
        read all of these; --export and x -T write "-sha256" after
        "-dense9". --export of a byte8 archive now writes a byte8
        transfer ("-sha256" and base64 of its octets, as encode_base64
        without -dense9) instead of a dense9 one; -9 and -8 choose.

0.06  2026-09-27
      - gzip, as the Multics gzip and gunzip commands write and read it:
        every key reads a gzipped archive (bit count from an MU extra
        subfield or a stored name ENTRY.BITCOUNT.dense9, checked against
        the archive), and changes are written back gzipped; --gzip makes
        a new archive NAME.archive.gz, or with --export a gzip file for
        Multics; --import also takes a gzip file or base64 of one; a name
        NAME.archive.BITCOUNT.dense9 (from gunzip -N) is read as is.
        Writing uses the Multics rule (BYTE8 unless there is 9-bit data)
        and OS code 3. Decompression is limited to one segment.
      - archive --name-bits: the x keys write binary components as
        NAME.BITCOUNT.dense9 (the name Multics gzip stores for DENSE9),
        and r, a and u take such a file as component NAME with that bit
        count, as if given --bits; with no paths, such files in the
        working directory are the sources. Off by default.
      - Archive::Multics::Component has its own POD (perldoc
        Archive::Multics::Component).

0.05  2026-09-25
      - dense9 archives: every 9 bits kept, 8 characters in 9 octets, as
        written on Multics by encode_base64 -dense9. A raw dense9 archive
        has the SHA-256 that sha256 -dense9 gives on Multics.
      - archive --import converts a transfer file ("-dense9 N" line,
        base64 body) to a raw dense9 archive; archive --export converts
        an archive to a transfer file for decode_base64, with the bit
        count computed from the contents. t and x also read transfer
        files; the keys that change an archive refuse them.
      - Binary components (9th bits set) extract as raw dense9, with the
        SHA-256 that sha256 -dense9 gives; -T/--transfer extracts them as
        transfer files, which record the bit count and can be put back
        bit for bit. x prints each binary's bit count, and --bits N puts
        a raw dense9 file back with r, a or u. A component with 9-bit
        data is not replaced from a file without a known bit count (a
        transfer file, or --bits): r and u refuse or skip it. -T is
        refused with keys other than x, and --bits with a transfer file.
        Text components extract as ordinary files, with the SHA-256
        that sha256 -byte8 gives.
      - archive(1): -9/--dense9 and -8/--byte8 choose the form written;
        -B/--bit-count prints the archive's bit count.
      - Archive::Multics: encoding and transfer options; bit_count,
        encoding, is_transfer, set_encoding, source_data; component
        has_ninth_bits, lossless, dense9 and transfer_string. A header
        with a 9th bit set is a format error.
      - Access modes round-trip: binary components keep execute
        permission when extracted (as text ones always did), and the
        mode recorded for an added file comes from its owner permission
        bits rather than effective access (which is always writable for
        root). A component extracted and put back unchanged keeps its
        mode and "modified" date; only "updated" changes.
      - Duplicate component names (joined or crafted archives): reading
        one warns; x extracts each copy in turn (previously the first
        copy's data every time, so later copies could not be extracted);
        xd and xdf delete only components whose file survives, so a copy
        is never lost, and repeated they take the copies out one at a
        time; r and u refuse a name that occurs more than once. New error
        code dupname.
      - Security hardening: a component name containing a control
        character is never used as a file name; archive text in error
        messages is shown with unprintable characters escaped; dense9
        input larger than one segment is refused before it is unpacked.

0.04  2026-09-23
      - Security: component names come from the archive and were used as
        file names unchecked. A crafted archive could make "archive x"
        (no names) write outside the working directory ("../name"), and
        make a global "archive r"/"u"/"rd"/"ud" read, and with the delete
        keys delete, a file outside it. Names that are empty, "." or "..",
        or contain "/" or NUL are now refused for extraction and skipped
        by global replace and update. Archive::Multics::safe_file_name()
        is the check; extract_component() refuses such a name as a
        default destination.

0.03  2026-09-23
      - Documentation: binary components (e.g. object segments) are corrupted
        by a text-mode transfer and are not detected; corrected the claim
        that object segments cannot be extracted.

0.02  2026-09-23
      - Recognize and ignore the copyright notice that the MIT Multics source
        site appends to its archive downloads.
      - New salvage option (archive -S): read damaged archives, e.g. with
        padding NULs lost in transfer, junk, truncation or trailing data.
      - Documented that there is no ac short name (ac(8) on OpenBSD).

0.01  2026-09-23
      - First release: Archive::Multics module and archive(1) command.
      - Reads and writes Multics archives in text mode; unchanged archives
        round-trip byte for byte.
      - archive(1) supports all 28 Multics keys, star names, and the
        Multics table layout and messages.
      - Two-digit years use the RFC 5322 cutoff (50).
      - pledge(2) and unveil(2) on OpenBSD.
